      "answer": "$(4,9)$",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=9-3(2x+1)^{1/2}$ — B1B1; one B1 for differentiating $9x$ and one for the chain-rule term. Including $+c$ makes the second term B0.\n$9-3(2x+1)^{1/2}=0$, leading to $2x+1=9$ — M1; set the derivative to zero and solve by squaring. Squaring the entire derivative equation incorrectly scores M0A0; sign errors such as $2x+1=\\pm9$ score M0.\nMaximum point $(4,9)$ — A1; working shown, and $y=9$ must come from the original equation.\nTotal: 4",
